You are asking:

If B is strongly related to A, why don't we simply use inheritance (B extends A)? Why put B inside A?

Because "strongly related" and "inherits from" mean two completely different relationships.

1. Inheritance means "IS-A"

If we write:

class Dog extends Animal {
}

we are saying:

Dog IS-A Animal.

Animal
   ↑
  Dog

A Dog is a specialized type of Animal.

So inheritance is about a type/parent-child relationship.

Other examples:

Car IS-A Vehicle
Dog IS-A Animal
Manager IS-A Employee
Circle IS-A Shape
2. Inner class means "BELONGS TO / IS PART OF"

Suppose:

class Car {

    class Engine {
    }
}

We are NOT saying:

Engine IS-A Car ❌

That would make no logical sense.

We're saying:

An Engine is a component/part of a Car.

Car
└── Engine

This is a has-a / part-of relationship.

3. This is the key difference
Inheritance
class Dog extends Animal

means:

Dog IS-A Animal
Inner class
class Car {
    class Engine {
    }
}

means:

Car HAS-A / contains an Engine concept

So:

extends
   ↓
IS-A relationship


inner class
   ↓
strongly associated / part-of relationship